{"name":"Velvet Umber","hex":"#6b605a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"velvet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A closely woven fabric (originally of silk, now also of cotton or man-made fibres) with a thick short pile on one side.","Very fine fur, including the skin and fur on a deer's antlers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with velvet or with a covering of a similar texture.","To coat raw meat in starch, then in oil, preparatory to frying."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/velvet"},{"term":"umber","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A brown clay, somewhat darker than ochre, which contains iron and manganese oxides.","Alternative form of umbrere."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a reddish brown colour, like that of the pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/umber"}],"entries":[{"title":"Velvet","blocks":[{"text":"Velvet is a type of woven fabric with a dense, even pile that gives it a distinctive soft feel. Historically, velvet was typically made from silk. Modern velvet can be made from silk, linen, cotton, wool, synthetic fibers, silk-cotton blends, or synthetic-natural fiber blends."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Blue_velvet_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Blue_velvet_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Velvet"},{"title":"Umber","blocks":[{"text":"Umber is a natural earth pigment consisting of iron oxide and manganese oxide; it has a brownish color that can vary among shades of yellow, red, and green. Umber is considered one of the oldest pigments known to humans. Umber's advantages are its highly versatile color, warm tone, and quick drying abilities. While some sources indicate that umber's name comes from its geographic origin in Umbria, other scholars suggest that it derives from the Latin word umbra, which means \"shadow\". The belief that its name derives from the word for shadow is fitting, as the color helps create shadows. The color is primarily produced in Cyprus. Umber is typically mined from open pits or underground mines and ground into a fine powder that is washed to remove impurities. In the 20th century, the rise of synthetic dyes decreased the demand for natural pigments such as umber."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Jules_Bastien-Lepage_-_Pas_M%C3%A8che_%28Nothing_Doing%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/330px-Jules_Bastien-Lepage_-_Pas_M%C3%A8che_%28Nothing_Doing%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Umber"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a9/Medici_velvet.jpg/330px-Medici_velvet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a9/Medici_velvet.jpg/330px-Medici_velvet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Velvet with Medici arms, Florence or Venice, 1440–1500","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMedici_velvet.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Chasuble_abondance.JPG/500px-Chasuble_abondance.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Chasuble_abondance.JPG/1280px-Chasuble_abondance.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Chasuble_abondance.JPG/500px-Chasuble_abondance.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A cope in pile-on-pile velvet","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChasuble_abondance.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Man%27s_Coat_and_Waistcoat_LACMA_M.57.35a-b_